\title{A study of a walker and an iterator for the WAVL containers}
\taxon{Session}
\date{2026-11-17}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{stdlib}
\meta{harness}{Claude Code}
\meta{model-id}{claude-fable-5-2}
\import{base-macros}

\p{After \pr{1049} the maintainer asked whether a zipper-based iterator or walker for \code{\verb~|WavlMap~} and \code{\verb~|WavlSet~} is feasible and worth having. A prototype for the map was written on the main branch at \pr{1049}, outside the repository, and timed against the existing operations and against \code{\verb~|CharSet~} as merged.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Design note.} [A walker and an iterator for WavlMap](design-wavl-walker), a draft with the prototype, the measurements and the open questions.}
\li{\strong{Result.} Feasible for the map with a small change, and about twice as fast for set algebra on \code{\verb~|CharSet~}; an iterator helps comparisons; a plain traversal and a long run of removals do not gain. The set was only analysed.}
\li{\strong{Pull requests.} The maintainer accepted the walker and the iterator for the map. They were first opened as one pull request, which also changed the repairs of \code{\verb~|insert~} and \code{\verb~|remove~}; the maintainer asked for that to be split, and it became [a stack of four](design-wavl-walker-stack), \pr{1051} to \pr{1054}, with \pr{1055} for CI over the whole. Each step builds and passes the WAVL test on its own. Changelog entry [log-H381](log-H381).}
\li{\strong{Not done.} The walker is not tuned. \code{\verb~|CharSet~} and the equality of maps still use the earlier operations. Local runs used a compiler built on 2026-11-13, older than the library it compiled.}
}

\p{\strong{The suite.} Asked whether the third and fourth steps are a gain, and told that the path form had made insertion worse, the session ran the ordered-map benchmarks of the benchmark suite for recursion, the main branch and the stack. The stack does not change them; the path form is slower than recursion for a map with one owner. Recorded in [the note](design-wavl-walker-suite) and as an insight listed under [insights](insights).}

\p{\strong{Decision.} On those results the maintainer had the change to the repairs and the changes through a walker closed, as not required for a walker: [dec-DR32](dec-DR32). \pr{1051} and \pr{1052} remain, with \pr{1056} for CI over both.}

\p{\strong{Regex.} \pr{1057} uses the iterator in \code{\verb~|CharSet~} and the UTF-8 translation, on top of \pr{1052}; changelog entry [log-2R2L](log-2R2L), measurements in [the note](design-wavl-walker-regex). \pr{1056}, opened only to run CI over two pull requests, was closed as pointless: a stacked pull request has its own CI.}

\p{\strong{Lists.} Asked whether the lists of entries in \code{\verb~|CharSet~} were needed, the session replaced the three of whole sets in \pr{1057} with folds and the iterator, and kept the three of a range, which measured as cheaper than stepping without them. The reads of \code{\verb~|cut~} that kept its map held became \pr{1058}, [log-YG59](log-YG59).}

\p{One timing was wrong at first and was repeated: the checksum of the window removals called \code{\verb~|depth~}, which visits every node, so both forms measured the same. The note has the repeated numbers.}
